{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019QCo9iopN2i1YnkSprmw8w","type":"tool_result","content":"# Debug Init Containers\n\n**Document Type:** Kubernetes Task Tutorial\n**License:** CC BY 4.0\n\n## Full Document Text\n\nThis page shows how to investigate problems related to the execution of Init Containers. The example command lines below refer to the Pod as `<pod-name>` and the Init Containers as `<init-container-1>` and `<init-container-2>`.\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nYou should be familiar with the basics of Init Containers and have configured an Init Container previously.\n\n### Checking the Status of Init Containers\n\nDisplay the status of your pod:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nFor example, a status of `Init:1/2` indicates that one of two Init Containers has completed successfully:\n\n```\nNAME         READY     STATUS     RESTARTS   AGE\n<pod-name>   0/1       Init:1/2   0          7s\n```\n\n### Getting Details About Init Containers\n\nView more detailed information about Init Container execution:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nExample output showing two Init Containers:\n\n```\nInit Containers:\n  <init-container-1>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Terminated\n      Reason:        Completed\n      Exit Code:     0\n    Ready:           True\n    Restart Count:   0\n  <init-container-2>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Waiting\n      Reason:        CrashLoopBackOff\n    Last State:      Terminated\n      Reason:        Error\n      Exit Code:     1\n    Ready:           False\n    Restart Count:   3\n```\n\nAccess Init Container statuses programmatically:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name> --template '{{.status.initContainerStatuses}}'\n```\n\n### Accessing Logs from Init Containers\n\nPass the Init Container name with the Pod name to access logs:\n\n```shell\nkubectl logs <pod-name> -c <init-container-2>\n```\n\nInit Containers running shell scripts print commands as executed. Enable this in Bash by running `set -x` at the script beginning.\n\n### Understanding Pod Status\n\nPod statuses beginning with `Init:` summarize Init Container execution status:\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `Init:N/M` | Pod has M Init Containers; N have completed |\n| `Init:Error` | An Init Container failed execution |\n| `Init:CrashLoopBackOff` | An Init Container failed repeatedly |\n| `Pending` | Pod has not begun executing Init Containers |\n| `PodInitializing` or `Running` | Pod finished executing Init Containers |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b156e7c-433e-416d-a6d5-5879b6834809","uuid":"52da9b22-eee9-4bdc-8623-76931a6f1765","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:48:25.918Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":3376,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Debug Init Containers\n\n**Document Type:** Kubernetes Task Tutorial\n**License:** CC BY 4.0\n\n## Full Document Text\n\nThis page shows how to investigate problems related to the execution of Init Containers. The example command lines below refer to the Pod as `<pod-name>` and the Init Containers as `<init-container-1>` and `<init-container-2>`.\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nYou should be familiar with the basics of Init Containers and have configured an Init Container previously.\n\n### Checking the Status of Init Containers\n\nDisplay the status of your pod:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nFor example, a status of `Init:1/2` indicates that one of two Init Containers has completed successfully:\n\n```\nNAME         READY     STATUS     RESTARTS   AGE\n<pod-name>   0/1       Init:1/2   0          7s\n```\n\n### Getting Details About Init Containers\n\nView more detailed information about Init Container execution:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nExample output showing two Init Containers:\n\n```\nInit Containers:\n  <init-container-1>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Terminated\n      Reason:        Completed\n      Exit Code:     0\n    Ready:           True\n    Restart Count:   0\n  <init-container-2>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Waiting\n      Reason:        CrashLoopBackOff\n    Last State:      Terminated\n      Reason:        Error\n      Exit Code:     1\n    Ready:           False\n    Restart Count:   3\n```\n\nAccess Init Container statuses programmatically:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name> --template '{{.status.initContainerStatuses}}'\n```\n\n### Accessing Logs from Init Containers\n\nPass the Init Container name with the Pod name to access logs:\n\n```shell\nkubectl logs <pod-name> -c <init-container-2>\n```\n\nInit Containers running shell scripts print commands as executed. Enable this in Bash by running `set -x` at the script beginning.\n\n### Understanding Pod Status\n\nPod statuses beginning with `Init:` summarize Init Container execution status:\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `Init:N/M` | Pod has M Init Containers; N have completed |\n| `Init:Error` | An Init Container failed execution |\n| `Init:CrashLoopBackOff` | An Init Container failed repeatedly |\n| `Pending` | Pod has not begun executing Init Containers |\n| `PodInitializing` or `Running` | Pod finished executing Init Containers |","durationMs":5925,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-init-containers.md"}}
