{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DPHqnzG1qU3TK8qmrQViPN","type":"tool_result","content":"===ENDPOINTSLICES===\nconcepts_covered: [\"endpointslice\", \"endpointslice-controller\", \"readiness-gated-membership\", \"terminating-endpoint\", \"service-name-label\", \"owner-reference\", \"manually-managed-endpointslice\"]\n> are retained where they qualify a condition's stability, because §4's Closer Look may need\n## Conditions\nThe EndpointSlice API stores conditions about endpoints that may be useful for consumers. The three conditions are `serving`, `terminating`, and `ready`.\n### Ready\nThe `ready` condition is essentially a shortcut for checking \"`serving` and not `terminating`\" (though it will also always be `true` for Services with `spec.publishNotReadyAddresses` set to `true`).\nThe `serving` condition indicates that the endpoint is currently serving responses, and so it should be used as a target for Service traffic. For endpoints backed by a Pod, this maps to the Pod's `Ready` condition.\n### Terminating\nThe `terminating` condition indicates that the endpoint is terminating. For endpoints backed by a Pod, this condition is set when the Pod is first deleted (that is, when it receives a deletion timestamp, but most likely before the Pod's containers exit).\nService proxies will normally ignore endpoints that are `terminating`, but they may route traffic to endpoints that are both `serving` and `terminating` if all available endpoints are `terminating`. (This helps to ensure that no Service traffic is lost during rolling updates of the underlying Pods.)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b156e7c-433e-416d-a6d5-5879b6834809","uuid":"648c9dea-3039-4e85-9c4d-27cee2b3e1de","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:50:01.756Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"===ENDPOINTSLICES===\nconcepts_covered: [\"endpointslice\", \"endpointslice-controller\", \"readiness-gated-membership\", \"terminating-endpoint\", \"service-name-label\", \"owner-reference\", \"manually-managed-endpointslice\"]\n> are retained where they qualify a condition's stability, because §4's Closer Look may need\n## Conditions\nThe EndpointSlice API stores conditions about endpoints that may be useful for consumers. The three conditions are `serving`, `terminating`, and `ready`.\n### Ready\nThe `ready` condition is essentially a shortcut for checking \"`serving` and not `terminating`\" (though it will also always be `true` for Services with `spec.publishNotReadyAddresses` set to `true`).\nThe `serving` condition indicates that the endpoint is currently serving responses, and so it should be used as a target for Service traffic. For endpoints backed by a Pod, this maps to the Pod's `Ready` condition.\n### Terminating\nThe `terminating` condition indicates that the endpoint is terminating. For endpoints backed by a Pod, this condition is set when the Pod is first deleted (that is, when it receives a deletion timestamp, but most likely before the Pod's containers exit).\nService proxies will normally ignore endpoints that are `terminating`, but they may route traffic to endpoints that are both `serving` and `terminating` if all available endpoints are `terminating`. (This helps to ensure that no Service traffic is lost during rolling updates of the underlying Pods.)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NwCXXL2hZDyei8c5SbiqtD","type":"tool_result","content":"# Developing and Debugging Services Locally Using Telepresence\n\nKubernetes applications typically consist of multiple services running in separate containers. Developing and debugging these services on a remote cluster can be challenging, often requiring shell access to running containers.\n\nTelepresence is a tool that simplifies this process by enabling local development and debugging while maintaining a connection to a remote Kubernetes cluster. It allows developers to use custom tools like debuggers and IDEs for local services while providing full access to ConfigMaps, secrets, and remote cluster services.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n* Kubernetes cluster is installed\n* `kubectl` is configured to communicate with the cluster\n* Telepresence is installed\n\n## Connecting Your Local Machine to a Remote Cluster\n\nAfter installing Telepresence, run `telepresence connect` to launch its Daemon and establish a connection:\n\n```\n$ telepresence connect\n\nLaunching Telepresence Daemon\n...\nConnected to context default (https://<cluster public IP>)\n```\n\nYou can then access services using Kubernetes syntax, such as `curl -ik https://kubernetes.default`\n\n## Developing or Debugging an Existing Service\n\n\"Use the `telepresence intercept $SERVICE_NAME --port $LOCAL_PORT:$REMOTE_PORT` command to create an 'intercept' for rerouting remote service traffic.\"\n\nThe parameters are:\n- `$SERVICE_NAME`: your local service name\n- `$LOCAL_PORT`: the port your service runs on locally\n- `$REMOTE_PORT`: the port your service listens to in the cluster\n\nThis command redirects remote traffic to your local service, enabling immediate visibility of code changes and facilitating debugging.\n\n## How Telepresence Works\n\nTelepresence deploys a traffic-agent sidecar alongside your application in the remote cluster. It intercepts incoming Pod traffic and routes either all traffic (global intercept) or a subset (personal intercept) to your local development environment instead of the remote application.\n\n## Next Steps\n\nFor hands-on experience, explore tutorials on developing services with Kubernetes on cloud platforms. Visit the Telepresence website for additional documentation and resources."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b156e7c-433e-416d-a6d5-5879b6834809","uuid":"5bfce854-0a8a-4619-8323-5b4f920955ac","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:50:02.303Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":3750,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Developing and Debugging Services Locally Using Telepresence\n\nKubernetes applications typically consist of multiple services running in separate containers. Developing and debugging these services on a remote cluster can be challenging, often requiring shell access to running containers.\n\nTelepresence is a tool that simplifies this process by enabling local development and debugging while maintaining a connection to a remote Kubernetes cluster. It allows developers to use custom tools like debuggers and IDEs for local services while providing full access to ConfigMaps, secrets, and remote cluster services.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n* Kubernetes cluster is installed\n* `kubectl` is configured to communicate with the cluster\n* Telepresence is installed\n\n## Connecting Your Local Machine to a Remote Cluster\n\nAfter installing Telepresence, run `telepresence connect` to launch its Daemon and establish a connection:\n\n```\n$ telepresence connect\n\nLaunching Telepresence Daemon\n...\nConnected to context default (https://<cluster public IP>)\n```\n\nYou can then access services using Kubernetes syntax, such as `curl -ik https://kubernetes.default`\n\n## Developing or Debugging an Existing Service\n\n\"Use the `telepresence intercept $SERVICE_NAME --port $LOCAL_PORT:$REMOTE_PORT` command to create an 'intercept' for rerouting remote service traffic.\"\n\nThe parameters are:\n- `$SERVICE_NAME`: your local service name\n- `$LOCAL_PORT`: the port your service runs on locally\n- `$REMOTE_PORT`: the port your service listens to in the cluster\n\nThis command redirects remote traffic to your local service, enabling immediate visibility of code changes and facilitating debugging.\n\n## How Telepresence Works\n\nTelepresence deploys a traffic-agent sidecar alongside your application in the remote cluster. It intercepts incoming Pod traffic and routes either all traffic (global intercept) or a subset (personal intercept) to your local development environment instead of the remote application.\n\n## Next Steps\n\nFor hands-on experience, explore tutorials on developing services with Kubernetes on cloud platforms. Visit the Telepresence website for additional documentation and resources.","durationMs":5018,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/local-debugging.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014aeL4KowZtRyrvN63DMUAi","type":"tool_result","content":"# Debug Init Containers\n\nThis page shows how to investigate problems related to Init Container execution. The example commands below refer to the Pod as `<pod-name>` and Init Containers as `<init-container-1>` and `<init-container-2>`.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n* Familiarity with the basics of Init Containers\n* A configured Init Container in a Pod\n\n## Checking the status of Init Containers\n\nDisplay your pod's status:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nFor example, a status of `Init:1/2` indicates one of two Init Containers has completed successfully:\n\n```\nNAME         READY     STATUS     RESTARTS   AGE\n<pod-name>   0/1       Init:1/2   0          7s\n```\n\n## Getting details about Init Containers\n\nView detailed information about Init Container execution:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nA Pod with two Init Containers might display:\n\n```\nInit Containers:\n  <init-container-1>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Terminated\n      Reason:        Completed\n      Exit Code:     0\n    Ready:           True\n    Restart Count:   0\n  <init-container-2>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Waiting\n      Reason:        CrashLoopBackOff\n    Last State:      Terminated\n      Reason:        Error\n      Exit Code:     1\n    Ready:           False\n    Restart Count:   3\n```\n\nAccess Init Container statuses programmatically:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name> --template '{{.status.initContainerStatuses}}'\n```\n\n## Accessing logs from Init Containers\n\nRetrieve logs by passing the Init Container name with the Pod name:\n\n```shell\nkubectl logs <pod-name> -c <init-container-2>\n```\n\n\"Init Containers that run a shell script print commands as they're executed.\"\n\n## Understanding Pod status\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `Init:N/M` | Pod has M Init Containers; N have completed |\n| `Init:Error` | An Init Container failed to execute |\n| `Init:CrashLoopBackOff` | An Init Container failed repeatedly |\n| `Pending` | Pod has not begun executing Init Containers |\n| `PodInitializing` or `Running` | Pod finished executing Init Containers |\n\n---\n*Attribution: Kubernetes Documentation (CC BY 4.0)*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b156e7c-433e-416d-a6d5-5879b6834809","uuid":"123fe27b-6e3b-4197-8242-6be80f15f63c","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:50:05.476Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":3376,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Debug Init Containers\n\nThis page shows how to investigate problems related to Init Container execution. The example commands below refer to the Pod as `<pod-name>` and Init Containers as `<init-container-1>` and `<init-container-2>`.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n* Familiarity with the basics of Init Containers\n* A configured Init Container in a Pod\n\n## Checking the status of Init Containers\n\nDisplay your pod's status:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nFor example, a status of `Init:1/2` indicates one of two Init Containers has completed successfully:\n\n```\nNAME         READY     STATUS     RESTARTS   AGE\n<pod-name>   0/1       Init:1/2   0          7s\n```\n\n## Getting details about Init Containers\n\nView detailed information about Init Container execution:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe pod <pod-name>\n```\n\nA Pod with two Init Containers might display:\n\n```\nInit Containers:\n  <init-container-1>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Terminated\n      Reason:        Completed\n      Exit Code:     0\n    Ready:           True\n    Restart Count:   0\n  <init-container-2>:\n    Container ID:    ...\n    State:           Waiting\n      Reason:        CrashLoopBackOff\n    Last State:      Terminated\n      Reason:        Error\n      Exit Code:     1\n    Ready:           False\n    Restart Count:   3\n```\n\nAccess Init Container statuses programmatically:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod <pod-name> --template '{{.status.initContainerStatuses}}'\n```\n\n## Accessing logs from Init Containers\n\nRetrieve logs by passing the Init Container name with the Pod name:\n\n```shell\nkubectl logs <pod-name> -c <init-container-2>\n```\n\n\"Init Containers that run a shell script print commands as they're executed.\"\n\n## Understanding Pod status\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `Init:N/M` | Pod has M Init Containers; N have completed |\n| `Init:Error` | An Init Container failed to execute |\n| `Init:CrashLoopBackOff` | An Init Container failed repeatedly |\n| `Pending` | Pod has not begun executing Init Containers |\n| `PodInitializing` or `Running` | Pod finished executing Init Containers |\n\n---\n*Attribution: Kubernetes Documentation (CC BY 4.0)*","durationMs":5411,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-init-containers.md"}}
