{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014rFkGTi2Mn8t9vJk9GGhW9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Get a Shell to a Running Container\n\n**License:** CC BY 4.0\n\n## Overview\n\nThis guide demonstrates how to use `kubectl exec` to access a shell within a running container.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nYou need a Kubernetes cluster and kubectl command-line tool installed and configured.\n\n## Getting a shell to a container\n\nCreate a Pod with a single container running nginx. Apply the configuration:\n\n```shell\nkubectl apply -f https://k8s.io/examples/application/shell-demo.yaml\n```\n\nVerify the container is running:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod shell-demo\n```\n\nAccess an interactive shell in the container:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec --stdin --tty shell-demo -- /bin/bash\n```\n\nThe double dash (`--`) separates kubectl arguments from the command arguments passed to the container.\n\nOnce inside the container shell, explore the filesystem:\n\n```shell\nls /\ncat /proc/mounts\ncat /proc/1/maps\n```\n\nYou can install and run diagnostic tools:\n\n```shell\napt-get update\napt-get install -y tcpdump\napt-get install -y lsof\napt-get install -y procps\nps aux\nps aux | grep nginx\n```\n\n## Writing the root page for nginx\n\nThe Pod configuration includes an `emptyDir` volume mounted at `/usr/share/nginx/html`. Create a custom index page:\n\n```shell\necho 'Hello shell demo' > /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html\n```\n\nTest the server:\n\n```shell\napt-get install curl\ncurl http://localhost/\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\nHello shell demo\n```\n\nExit the container shell by typing `exit`.\n\n## Running individual commands in a container\n\nExecute commands without entering an interactive shell. List environment variables:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- env\n```\n\nRun other commands directly:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- ps aux\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- ls /\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- cat /proc/1/mounts\n```\n\n## Opening a shell when a Pod has more than one container\n\nWhen a Pod contains multiple containers, specify the target container using the `--container` or `-c` flag:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec -i -t my-pod --container main-app -- /bin/bash\n```\n\nThe short options `-i` and `-t` correspond to `--stdin` and `--tty`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b156e7c-433e-416d-a6d5-5879b6834809","uuid":"b9c4ad2d-96fd-4942-b27b-fb826f47e21b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:50:28.357Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":3210,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Get a Shell to a Running Container\n\n**License:** CC BY 4.0\n\n## Overview\n\nThis guide demonstrates how to use `kubectl exec` to access a shell within a running container.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nYou need a Kubernetes cluster and kubectl command-line tool installed and configured.\n\n## Getting a shell to a container\n\nCreate a Pod with a single container running nginx. Apply the configuration:\n\n```shell\nkubectl apply -f https://k8s.io/examples/application/shell-demo.yaml\n```\n\nVerify the container is running:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pod shell-demo\n```\n\nAccess an interactive shell in the container:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec --stdin --tty shell-demo -- /bin/bash\n```\n\nThe double dash (`--`) separates kubectl arguments from the command arguments passed to the container.\n\nOnce inside the container shell, explore the filesystem:\n\n```shell\nls /\ncat /proc/mounts\ncat /proc/1/maps\n```\n\nYou can install and run diagnostic tools:\n\n```shell\napt-get update\napt-get install -y tcpdump\napt-get install -y lsof\napt-get install -y procps\nps aux\nps aux | grep nginx\n```\n\n## Writing the root page for nginx\n\nThe Pod configuration includes an `emptyDir` volume mounted at `/usr/share/nginx/html`. Create a custom index page:\n\n```shell\necho 'Hello shell demo' > /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html\n```\n\nTest the server:\n\n```shell\napt-get install curl\ncurl http://localhost/\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\nHello shell demo\n```\n\nExit the container shell by typing `exit`.\n\n## Running individual commands in a container\n\nExecute commands without entering an interactive shell. List environment variables:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- env\n```\n\nRun other commands directly:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- ps aux\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- ls /\nkubectl exec shell-demo -- cat /proc/1/mounts\n```\n\n## Opening a shell when a Pod has more than one container\n\nWhen a Pod contains multiple containers, specify the target container using the `--container` or `-c` flag:\n\n```shell\nkubectl exec -i -t my-pod --container main-app -- /bin/bash\n```\n\nThe short options `-i` and `-t` correspond to `--stdin` and `--tty`.","durationMs":5252,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/get-shell-running-container.md"}}
