{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RvrESkVsXYncUN6TpDSj9J","type":"tool_result","content":"# kubectl debug Flags\n\n## --profile Flag\n\n**Full description:**\n\"Options are \"general\", \"baseline\", \"restricted\", \"netadmin\" or \"sysadmin\". Defaults to \"general\"\"\n\n**Profile names listed:** general, baseline, restricted, netadmin, sysadmin\n\n**Default:** general\n\n## --custom Flag\n\n**Full description:**\n\"Path to a JSON or YAML file containing a partial container spec to customize built-in debug profiles.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b156e7c-433e-416d-a6d5-5879b6834809","uuid":"103aa348-7421-47cf-86d4-46b612380a8b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:51:12.180Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":506322,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# kubectl debug Flags\n\n## --profile Flag\n\n**Full description:**\n\"Options are \"general\", \"baseline\", \"restricted\", \"netadmin\" or \"sysadmin\". Defaults to \"general\"\"\n\n**Profile names listed:** general, baseline, restricted, netadmin, sysadmin\n\n**Default:** general\n\n## --custom Flag\n\n**Full description:**\n\"Path to a JSON or YAML file containing a partial container spec to customize built-in debug profiles.\"","durationMs":2660,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_debug/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0152p9q5QKp8sbh7NYAe6td6","type":"tool_result","content":"# Debug Pods\n\nThis guide helps users troubleshoot applications deployed in Kubernetes that aren't functioning correctly. This is *not* a guide for debugging your cluster—see the separate cluster debugging guide for that.\n\n## Diagnosing the Problem\n\nTroubleshooting starts with triage. Identify whether the issue involves your Pods, Replication Controller, or Service:\n\n* Debugging Pods\n* Debugging Replication Controllers\n* Debugging Services\n\n### Debugging Pods\n\nBegin by examining the Pod's current state and recent events:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe pods ${POD_NAME}\n```\n\nCheck whether all containers are in `Running` state and review any recent restarts.\n\n#### My pod stays pending\n\nWhen a Pod remains stuck in `Pending`, it cannot be scheduled onto a node, typically due to insufficient resources. The `kubectl describe` output should show scheduler messages explaining the scheduling failure. Common reasons include:\n\n* **Insufficient resources**: You may have exhausted CPU or Memory capacity. Delete Pods, adjust resource requests, or add nodes to your cluster.\n\n* **Using hostPort**: Binding to `hostPort` limits scheduling locations. \"In most cases, `hostPort` is unnecessary\" and a Service object is preferable. Pod count cannot exceed your cluster's node count when using `hostPort`.\n\n#### My pod stays waiting\n\nA Pod stuck in `Waiting` has been scheduled to a worker node but cannot run there. The `kubectl describe` output provides diagnostic information. \"The most common cause of `Waiting` pods is a failure to pull the image.\" Check these three items:\n\n* Verify the image name is correct\n* Confirm you've pushed the image to the registry\n* Manually pull the image to test accessibility (e.g., `docker pull <image>`)\n\n#### My pod stays terminating\n\nA Pod stuck in `Terminating` means deletion was requested but the control plane cannot remove it. This typically occurs when a finalizer exists and an admission webhook prevents finalizer removal.\n\nCheck whether ValidatingWebhookConfiguration or MutatingWebhookConfiguration target `UPDATE` operations for `pods` resources.\n\nFor third-party webhooks:\n- Update to the latest version\n- Disable the webhook for `UPDATE` operations\n- Report the issue to the provider\n\nFor your own webhook:\n- Mutating webhooks must never change immutable fields during `UPDATE` operations\n- Validating webhooks should allow Pods with existing violations to pass validation, permitting Pods created before webhook installation to continue running\n\n#### My pod is crashing or otherwise unhealthy\n\nOnce scheduled, use the methods described in \"Debug Running Pods\" for further troubleshooting.\n\n#### My pod is running but not doing what I told it to do\n\nIf your Pod behaves unexpectedly, errors in your Pod description may have been silently ignored during creation. \"Often a section of the pod description is nested incorrectly, or a key name is typed incorrectly, and so the key is ignored.\"\n\nDelete your Pod and recreate it using validation:\n\n```shell\nkubectl apply --validate -f mypod.yaml\n```\n\nTypos like `commnd` instead of `command` produce errors such as:\n\n```shell\nI0805 10:43:25.129850   46757 schema.go:126] unknown field: commnd\nI0805 10:43:25.129973   46757 schema.go:129] this may be a false alarm\npods/mypod\n```\n\nNext, compare your local Pod specification with what exists on the API server:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pods/mypod -o yaml > mypod-on-apiserver.yaml\n```\n\nManually compare your original `mypod.yaml` with `mypod-on-apiserver.yaml`. The API server version typically contains additional lines (expected behavior). However, if lines present in your original are absent from the API server version, this may indicate a Pod specification problem.\n\n### Debugging Replication Controllers\n\nReplication controllers either create Pods or they don't. If creation fails, refer to the Pod debugging instructions above.\n\nUse this command to review replication controller events:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe rc ${CONTROLLER_NAME}\n```\n\n### Debugging Services\n\nServices load-balance across Pod sets. Several common issues prevent proper Service operation.\n\nFirst, verify that Service endpoints exist by checking EndpointSlice resources:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=${SERVICE_NAME}\n```\n\nEnsure endpoint count matches your expected Pod membership. For example, an nginx Service with 3 replicas should show three distinct IP addresses in EndpointSlices.\n\n#### My service is missing endpoints\n\nList Pods matching the Service's selector labels:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pods --selector=name=nginx,type=frontend\n```\n\nVerify the list matches expected Pods and confirm each Pod's `containerPort` matches the Service's `targetPort`.\n\n#### Network traffic is not forwarded\n\nConsult the service debugging guide for additional information.\n\n## What's Next\n\nIf these steps don't resolve your issue, follow the Debugging Service document to verify your Service runs, has Endpoints, Pods serve correctly, DNS functions, iptables rules exist, and kube-proxy behaves normally.\n\nSee the troubleshooting document for additional information."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b156e7c-433e-416d-a6d5-5879b6834809","uuid":"16c7dbb4-f910-4880-a746-f74479dade1d","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:51:23.600Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":8225,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Debug Pods\n\nThis guide helps users troubleshoot applications deployed in Kubernetes that aren't functioning correctly. This is *not* a guide for debugging your cluster—see the separate cluster debugging guide for that.\n\n## Diagnosing the Problem\n\nTroubleshooting starts with triage. Identify whether the issue involves your Pods, Replication Controller, or Service:\n\n* Debugging Pods\n* Debugging Replication Controllers\n* Debugging Services\n\n### Debugging Pods\n\nBegin by examining the Pod's current state and recent events:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe pods ${POD_NAME}\n```\n\nCheck whether all containers are in `Running` state and review any recent restarts.\n\n#### My pod stays pending\n\nWhen a Pod remains stuck in `Pending`, it cannot be scheduled onto a node, typically due to insufficient resources. The `kubectl describe` output should show scheduler messages explaining the scheduling failure. Common reasons include:\n\n* **Insufficient resources**: You may have exhausted CPU or Memory capacity. Delete Pods, adjust resource requests, or add nodes to your cluster.\n\n* **Using hostPort**: Binding to `hostPort` limits scheduling locations. \"In most cases, `hostPort` is unnecessary\" and a Service object is preferable. Pod count cannot exceed your cluster's node count when using `hostPort`.\n\n#### My pod stays waiting\n\nA Pod stuck in `Waiting` has been scheduled to a worker node but cannot run there. The `kubectl describe` output provides diagnostic information. \"The most common cause of `Waiting` pods is a failure to pull the image.\" Check these three items:\n\n* Verify the image name is correct\n* Confirm you've pushed the image to the registry\n* Manually pull the image to test accessibility (e.g., `docker pull <image>`)\n\n#### My pod stays terminating\n\nA Pod stuck in `Terminating` means deletion was requested but the control plane cannot remove it. This typically occurs when a finalizer exists and an admission webhook prevents finalizer removal.\n\nCheck whether ValidatingWebhookConfiguration or MutatingWebhookConfiguration target `UPDATE` operations for `pods` resources.\n\nFor third-party webhooks:\n- Update to the latest version\n- Disable the webhook for `UPDATE` operations\n- Report the issue to the provider\n\nFor your own webhook:\n- Mutating webhooks must never change immutable fields during `UPDATE` operations\n- Validating webhooks should allow Pods with existing violations to pass validation, permitting Pods created before webhook installation to continue running\n\n#### My pod is crashing or otherwise unhealthy\n\nOnce scheduled, use the methods described in \"Debug Running Pods\" for further troubleshooting.\n\n#### My pod is running but not doing what I told it to do\n\nIf your Pod behaves unexpectedly, errors in your Pod description may have been silently ignored during creation. \"Often a section of the pod description is nested incorrectly, or a key name is typed incorrectly, and so the key is ignored.\"\n\nDelete your Pod and recreate it using validation:\n\n```shell\nkubectl apply --validate -f mypod.yaml\n```\n\nTypos like `commnd` instead of `command` produce errors such as:\n\n```shell\nI0805 10:43:25.129850   46757 schema.go:126] unknown field: commnd\nI0805 10:43:25.129973   46757 schema.go:129] this may be a false alarm\npods/mypod\n```\n\nNext, compare your local Pod specification with what exists on the API server:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pods/mypod -o yaml > mypod-on-apiserver.yaml\n```\n\nManually compare your original `mypod.yaml` with `mypod-on-apiserver.yaml`. The API server version typically contains additional lines (expected behavior). However, if lines present in your original are absent from the API server version, this may indicate a Pod specification problem.\n\n### Debugging Replication Controllers\n\nReplication controllers either create Pods or they don't. If creation fails, refer to the Pod debugging instructions above.\n\nUse this command to review replication controller events:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe rc ${CONTROLLER_NAME}\n```\n\n### Debugging Services\n\nServices load-balance across Pod sets. Several common issues prevent proper Service operation.\n\nFirst, verify that Service endpoints exist by checking EndpointSlice resources:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=${SERVICE_NAME}\n```\n\nEnsure endpoint count matches your expected Pod membership. For example, an nginx Service with 3 replicas should show three distinct IP addresses in EndpointSlices.\n\n#### My service is missing endpoints\n\nList Pods matching the Service's selector labels:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get pods --selector=name=nginx,type=frontend\n```\n\nVerify the list matches expected Pods and confirm each Pod's `containerPort` matches the Service's `targetPort`.\n\n#### Network traffic is not forwarded\n\nConsult the service debugging guide for additional information.\n\n## What's Next\n\nIf these steps don't resolve your issue, follow the Debugging Service document to verify your Service runs, has Endpoints, Pods serve correctly, DNS functions, iptables rules exist, and kube-proxy behaves normally.\n\nSee the troubleshooting document for additional information.","durationMs":15833,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-pods.md"}}
